WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.611 --> 00:00:08.407
"August 8th 1940 and the battle for Britain
is on..."

00:00:08.574 --> 00:00:11.621
"30 enemy aircraft over the Channel flying
due West!"

00:00:11.826 --> 00:00:16.290
It would become one of the most significant
battles of the Second World War.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:20.794
Advancing through Europe, Nazi Germany set
its sights on the British Isles.

00:00:20.794 --> 00:00:26.258
"Phase one, knock out the Royal Air Force
and it's bases, get control of the air and

00:00:26.258 --> 00:00:28.545
the sea lanes across the Channel"

00:00:30.054 --> 00:00:33.724
Outnumbered and outgunned, it looked like
British fighters wouldn't stand a chance

00:00:33.724 --> 00:00:35.809
against the superior Luftwaffe.

00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:39.063
But they had a technology that would give
them an edge...

00:00:40.981 --> 00:00:44.568
Here's How Britain's Hunt For A Death
Ray Gave Us Radar

00:00:45.819 --> 00:00:49.239
In 2017, something incredible happened.

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:55.245
Not a single passenger jet was involved in
a fatal accident, despite a record four billion

00:00:55.245 --> 00:00:57.289
air passenger journeys being made.

00:00:58.457 --> 00:01:04.872
The skies had never been so busy - but 2017
was the safest year in commercial aviation.

00:01:05.214 --> 00:01:08.592
In fact, you're much more at risk on the
way to the airport.

00:01:08.592 --> 00:01:13.972
According to a Harvard study, the odds of
being killed in a car crash are 1 in 5000.

00:01:13.972 --> 00:01:18.143
Your odds of death in a plane crash are 1
in 11 million.

00:01:18.143 --> 00:01:21.647
How aircraft avoid mid-air collisions is thanks
to radar.

00:01:21.647 --> 00:01:25.901
But it all started with the search for a deadly
weapon.

00:01:25.901 --> 00:01:27.668
Roll back to 1935.

00:01:27.689 --> 00:01:32.699
War was looming and there were rumours Germany
had something capable of wiping out whole towns

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.620
The British Government feared an aerial invasion
and wanted a 'death ray' that could knock

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:38.831
German planes out of the sky.

00:01:38.831 --> 00:01:43.085
The Air Ministry approached electrical engineer,
Robert Watson-Watt.

00:01:43.419 --> 00:01:47.756
Watson-Watt had worked for the Meteorological
Office where he experimented using radio waves

00:01:47.756 --> 00:01:49.550
to locate thunderstorms.

00:01:49.716 --> 00:01:53.387
He quickly determined a death ray was practically
impossible to build.

00:01:53.387 --> 00:01:57.474
But leaning on their previous work in firing
radio waves at targets,

00:01:57.516 --> 00:01:59.726
they offered an alternative to the death ray.

00:02:00.102 --> 00:02:05.315
His memo to the Ministry was titled 
"The Detection of Aircraft by Radio Methods"

00:02:05.315 --> 00:02:10.654
Two weeks later, the team successfully used
a radio transmitter to detect a circling RAF

00:02:10.654 --> 00:02:13.031
bomber 8 miles away.

00:02:13.031 --> 00:02:17.244
Radio detection and ranging - radar - was
born.

00:02:17.244 --> 00:02:21.874
The team quickly extended the range of radar
up to 100 miles.

00:02:21.874 --> 00:02:26.462
They worked fast - the fear of imminent attack
was never far from their minds.

00:02:26.462 --> 00:02:32.301
By the outbreak of war in 1939 the team had built
19 radar stations on Britain's south and

00:02:32.301 --> 00:02:36.346
east coasts, with over 50 built by 1945.

00:02:36.388 --> 00:02:41.310
This system of stations became known as 'Chain
Home'; as well as detecting enemy aircraft,

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:45.814
it centralized radar reports to operators
who could quickly scramble fighter crews to

00:02:45.814 --> 00:02:47.524
intercept the enemy.

00:02:47.524 --> 00:02:51.278
Teams from Germany, Japan and the U.S. were
all working on their own radar systems'

00:02:51.278 --> 00:02:54.156
but the U.K.'s was the most advanced.

00:02:54.156 --> 00:02:57.659
British scientists developed something called
a 'cavity magnetron'

00:02:57.659 --> 00:03:02.331
improving on a German idea - and then shared it with allies in the U.S.

00:03:02.331 --> 00:03:07.503
The technology reduced the size of radar devices
meaning they could be installed in night-fighters,

00:03:07.503 --> 00:03:10.589
anti-submarine aircraft and escort ships.

00:03:10.589 --> 00:03:15.886
Planes were given transponders - devices that
send and receive radio signals - which would

00:03:15.928 --> 00:03:21.308
help them identify enemy and friendly planes,
increasingly useful when visibility was poor.

00:03:21.308 --> 00:03:23.727
But radar could only do so much.

00:03:23.727 --> 00:03:26.939
"The Nazis struck Britain with all their
might.

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:32.236
They levelled thousands upon thousands of
homes and damaged millions of others.

00:03:32.236 --> 00:03:35.906
They killed more than 40,000 men, women and
children."

00:03:35.906 --> 00:03:38.700
Without radar, it might have been much worse.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:42.246
The Nazis never landed on British soil.

00:03:43.455 --> 00:03:47.417
After the war, air travel exploded, especially
in the U.S.

00:03:47.876 --> 00:03:53.674
"Many other war-tested devices are incorporated
in the luxury airliner which ushers in a new

00:03:53.674 --> 00:03:55.467
era in American aviation"

00:03:56.341 --> 00:04:00.722
But there were few regulations and many airports
didn't even install radar

00:04:00.722 --> 00:04:04.184
"See And Be Seen" was the main rule of thumb.

00:04:04.184 --> 00:04:07.938
That would quickly change after June 30 1956.

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:15.445
128 people died following a collision between
two airlines over the Grand Canyon.

00:04:15.445 --> 00:04:20.242
Investigators determined the pilots could
not see each other due to cloudy conditions.

00:04:20.242 --> 00:04:24.204
As the public learned of the crude nature
of air traffic control, pressure mounted on

00:04:24.204 --> 00:04:26.582
Congress to make air travel safer.

00:04:26.582 --> 00:04:31.670
A year later President Eisenhower signed the
Airways Modernization Act, which improved

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:35.090
monitoring of planes from takeoff to landing.

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:39.553
Using transponders based on those developed
during the Battle of Britain, US airlines

00:04:39.553 --> 00:04:43.932
devised a compulsory system that would become
industry-standard known as TCAS -

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:46.268
the Traffic Alert and Collision Avoidance System.

00:04:46.268 --> 00:04:51.064
If a plane entered another's air space,
it would automatically issue a command to manoeuvre.

00:04:51.064 --> 00:04:52.399
CLIMB CLIMB,

00:04:52.649 --> 00:04:54.526
DESCEND, DESCEND

00:04:54.985 --> 00:04:59.323
Today, radar systems work in conjunction to
paint a complete and detailed picture of the

00:04:59.323 --> 00:05:05.287
skies, using transponders to identify planes,
their positions and their altitude.

00:05:05.287 --> 00:05:09.541
Air traffic control on the ground tracks speed
and direction by monitoring transmissions.

00:05:09.541 --> 00:05:15.925
"As the controllers say, 'the best way to
fly is in radar contact'"

00:05:17.799 --> 00:05:24.139
GPS has been added to the equation too because
once an aircraft is out at sea, radar coverage fades.

00:05:24.139 --> 00:05:27.559
Planes can now fly for hours with precise
navigation.

00:05:27.559 --> 00:05:33.565
So events like the disappearance of Malaysian
Airways flight MH370 in 2014 undoubtedly add

00:05:33.565 --> 00:05:35.901
to the fears we have of flying,

00:05:36.351 --> 00:05:41.239
but when looked at in context, such accidents
are extremely rare thanks to the giant leaps

00:05:41.239 --> 00:05:46.328
made in safety, galvanised all those years
ago by the Battle of Britain.

